.TH RESTOR 1M
.SH NAME
restor \- incremental file system restore
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B restor
key [ arguments ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B restor
is used exclusively to read any medium dumped with the
.B dump
command, and restore it to a file system on disk.
The default device
read from is floppy drive 0
.RB ( /dev/f0 )
on PRO/VENIX,
RAINBOW/VENIX, and VENIX/11 on Micro PDP-11 systems; it is
magtape drive 0
.RB ( /dev/mt0 )
on other VENIX/11 systems.
This default
may be overridden with the
.B f
modifier (see below).
.PP
The
.I key
specifies what is to be done.
.I key
is one or more of the following
characters:
.TP 6
.B t
The date that the medium was made and the date that was
specified in the
.B dump
command are printed.
A list of all the i-numbers
on the medium is also given.
.TP
.B r
The medium is read and loaded into the file system specified in
.IR arguments .
This should not be done lightly (see below).
.TP
.B x
Each file on the medium is individually extracted into a file
whose name is the file's i-number.
If there are
.IR arguments ,
they
are interpreted as i-numbers and only they are extracted.
.TP
.B c
If the medium overflows, increment the last character of its name
and continue on that drive.
(Normally it asks you to change
media).
.TP
.B f
Read the dump from the next argument file instead of the default
.RB ( /dev/f0
for PRO/VENIX and RAINBOW/VENIX;
.B /dev/mt0
for VENIX/11).
.TP
.B i
All read and checksum errors are reported, but will not cause
termination.
.TP
.B w
In conjunction with the
.B x
option, before each file is extracted, its
i-number is typed out.
To extract this file, you must respond
with a
.BR y .
.PP
The
.B x
option is used to retrieve individual files.
If the i-number of the
desired file is not known, it can be discovered by following the file system
directory search algorithm.
First retrieve the
.I root
directory whose i-number
is 1.
List this file with
.BR "ls \-fi 1" .
This will give names and i-numbers
of sub-directories.
Iterating, any file may be retrieved.
.PP
The
.B r
option should only be used to restore a complete dump medium
onto a clear file system or to restore an incremental dump medium onto
this.
.RB ( restor
will ask you for confirmation before writing over the file system;
hit CR to continue).
.PP
A
.B dump
followed by a
.B mkfs
and a
.B restor
can be used to save files,
increase the size of a file system, and restore the files.
The file system
.BR restor ed
should be the same size or larger than the original file system.
.SH EXAMPLES
Make new file system on
.IR /dev/w0.usr ,
and restore from the default
device:
.PP
.RS
.nf
/etc/mkfs /dev/w0.usr 13740
restor r /dev/w0.usr
.fi
.RE
.PP
Another
.B restor
can be done to get an incremental dump in on top of this.
.SH FILES
.nf
.ta 1i
/dev/f0	(PRO/VENIX and RAINBOW/VENIX default)
/dev/mt0	(VENIX/11 default)
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
dump(1m), mkfs(1m)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
There are various diagnostics involved with reading the media and writing
the disk.
There are also diagnostics if the i-list or the free list of the
file system is not large enough to hold the dump.
.PP
If the dump extends over more than one medium, it may ask you to
change media.
Reply with a newline when the next medium has been
mounted.
.SH BUGS
There is redundant information on the medium that could be used in case
of reading problems.
Unfortunately,
.B restor
doesn't use it.
.sp
